    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This work addresses the relationship between viscoelasticity and development of stress in cooling basalt. Significant research into the formation of polygonal cooling joint in basalt has led to an understanding of the formation mechanism of fractures, but past modeling of stress treated the material as purely elastic below its glass transition temperature. Using field measurements of a representative basalt flow at Box Canyon, Idaho, we developed a thermal model to estimate the cooling history for the basalt. The thermal strain resulting from this cooling is used as a boundary condition in a transient numerical model using a viscoelastic plate approximation. The development of stress in the basalt is analyzed over time at different depths within the basalt flow. An important distinction of our model is that the viscosity of the material is treated as a temperature dependent parameter, and the effects of viscoelastic relaxation are included. The results demonstrate that faster cooling and higher strain rates lead to higher effective glass transition temperatures, consistent with previous suggestions by researchers. The total thermal strain in the system is a constant, yet the percentage of strain resulting in stress that is not relaxed by viscous flow varies with position in the basalt flow. This strain partitioning may act in conjunction with the theory that fractures spacing variation in basalt is the result of longer fractures inhibiting the growth of shorter neighbors, leading to larger fracture spacing in the interior of basalt flows.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
